{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION to this ticket's premise — the trigger is not `--caused-by`\n\nI filed this as \"close `--caused-by` adds a duplicate edge\". **`rebar transition` has no\n`--caused-by` flag**, and unknown flags are silently ignored — proven on a live ticket with no\nmutation:\n\n```\n$ rebar transition major-specified-bream open open --definitely-not-a-real-flag\nno change: 50e3-6d2a-0e7f-4e92 already open      # exit 0, no error\n```\n\n`rebar transition --help` lists only `--class`, `--reason`, `--force`, `--output`. Filed separately\nas `brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee` (`0276-7eea-baf0-48e3`, P1), since the flag IS documented in\n`docs/user-guide.md:194`, `docs/clone-guidance.md` and the `/rebar-debug` skill.\n\n## The corrected mechanism\n\nEvery duplicate observed today was:\n\n1. an edge written earlier by an explicit `rebar link <bug> <origin> caused_by`; **plus**\n2. an edge written by **blame auto-derivation running on close** — which does not check whether a\n   `caused_by` edge is already recorded.\n\nThe flag I believed was creating the second edge was inert throughout. So the defect is\n**\"blame auto-derivation adds an edge without reconciling against existing ones\"**, and it is\ntriggered by the far more common sequence `rebar link` → close, with no flag involved at all.\n\nThat makes the bug **easier to hit than filed**: it needs only that someone recorded the origin\nbefore closing, which the `/rebar-debug` protocol explicitly encourages (\"it gets the finding down\nbefore Phase 2 can be interrupted\").\n\n## The fix in review (change 1973) is still correct\n\n`_apply_caused_by` is where the blame-derived culprit lands, so making it reconcile against\nalready-recorded edges fixes the real trigger, not just the imagined one. No rework needed on that\nbasis.\n\nOne consequence for **AC3** (a different origin must not be silently dropped): that path is\ncurrently **unreachable from the CLI**, since nothing populates an explicit culprit there. It\nremains reachable from the library/MCP seam (`_lib_writes.py:339` threads `caused_by=`), so the\ncriterion is still meaningful and worth keeping — but it should be tested at the library seam\nrather than through a CLI flag that does not exist. Once `brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee` implements\nthe flag, the CLI path becomes reachable and that test covers it too.\n\n## An honest note on the evidence in my earlier comment\n\nMy second comment argued the guidance to always pass the flag must stand, because an empty flag\nlets blame attach a wrong edge. **The premise was right but the illustration was wrong**: since the\nflag never worked, blame ran on all four closes anyway — and in every observed case it landed on\n**the same origin the agent had proven by hand**. So today's data says blame was redundant here,\nnot wrong.\n\nThe argument for supplying a proven origin still holds on principle. It just is not supported by\nthe examples I gave, and I have corrected the related ticket\n(`major-specified-bream`, `50e3-6d2a-0e7f-4e92`) which over-claimed on the same mistake."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787270545881494001,"uuid":"2cd73744-6fd9-411c-a60d-fee9d3db2c75"}